{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Knows, etc.</b> He mentioned the ox and the donkey because they are constantly found among people." "<b>Ebus.</b> The place of its eating. It is in the construct state and as an adjective is equivalent, and from it 'and fattened fowl in stalls' (1 Kings 8:3 [n]) and 'of stalled ox' (Proverbs 15:17) — a po'el form, and the meaning is that it stands in the stall." "<b>Israel does not know.</b> Behold, the beasts are better in knowledge than he." "<b>And My people does not understand.</b> The meaning is doubled, as is the way of most prophecies, and the Song of Ha'azinu, and also the parables of Balaam. And the meaning: for I have reared them, yet they did not know me."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {1 "Ibn Ezra cites 1 Kings 5:3 in the Hebrew verse numbering (= 1 Kings 4:23 in English numbering); 8:3 is likely a typographical error in the source."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:51:27.537188110Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}